# ADR-003: Query AcoustID with the candidate track's known duration, not the clip's

## Status

Accepted (2026-07-31)

## Context

Real-world line-in testing (see `DEVLOG.md`, 2026-07-20/21) found a ~20% hit
rate (1 match out of 5 albums tried) via the Focusrite. Every miss was
diagnosed the same way: technically clean recording, human-confirmed correct
track, zero results from AcoustID's raw lookup at every clip length tried
(15-90s). Track-boundary contamination and API/account health were both
ruled out directly. The working conclusion in the DEVLOG at the time was
that this reflected genuine, uneven coverage gaps in AcoustID's crowdsourced
fingerprint database for CD-sourced audio - a real limitation to design
around, not a bug to fix.

That conclusion was wrong, and testing against clean digital rips of the
three specific albums that had failed physically (GNX, Grace, The Low End
Theory - provided as m4a files) proved it. Fingerprinting those exact
tracks and sending them to AcoustID's raw lookup API succeeded immediately,
at 0.96-0.99 confidence. AcoustID has these recordings well-fingerprinted.
The coverage-gap theory was falsified outright.

Digging into *why* our own pipeline still failed on identical audio led to
the real mechanism: AcoustID's `duration` parameter in the lookup request
isn't just metadata - it materially affects matching. Isolated with a
single fixed fingerprint (same bytes, from a genuinely 60-second real clip
of "Luther"):

| Declared duration | Result |
|---|---|
| 60 (honest, matches the clip) | 0 results |
| 90, 120, 240, 300 | 0 results |
| 178 (the track's *true* length, which we already had stored from Discogs) | **0.99 confidence match** |

Only declaring a duration close to the true reference recording's length
worked - not "any large number," specifically the right ballpark. The same
pattern held for all three tracks: Mojo Pin (stored: 342s) and Rap Promoter
(stored: 133s) both matched at 0.96+ confidence once their own stored
durations were declared, using clips as short as 15-20 seconds of real
audio. `identify.py`'s entire escalating-clip-length design (ADR-002 layer
4) had been sending the clip's own honest (short) duration the whole time -
exactly the case that reliably fails.

## Decision

When looking up a clip via AcoustID, try the lookup once per each of the
active album's *known* track durations (already stored in our own `tracks`
table from Discogs/MusicBrainz), instead of the clip's actual recorded
length. Concretely:

1. Split fingerprinting from lookup: `fingerprint.compressed_fingerprint()`
   produces the fingerprint alone; `fingerprint.lookup_with_duration()`
   takes an explicit duration to declare, independent of the clip's real
   length.
2. `identify.identify_current_track()` fingerprints the recorded clip once,
   then loops over the album's known track durations (deduplicated, plus
   the clip's own honest duration as a last-resort fallback) calling the
   lookup with each, checking the album-constrained fuzzy matcher (ADR-002
   layer 4) against each response.
3. Clip length escalation (ADR-002's 30s/60s/90s) still exists as an outer
   loop, but now mostly as a safety net for cases where no stored duration
   is correct (e.g. a wrong tracklist edition) - not the primary lever.
   Default clip lengths dropped to 20s/45s/90s, since 15-20s of real audio
   is enough once the duration guess is right.

## Consequences

- Retracts part of ADR-002's own conclusion: the original "duration is the
  dominant bottleneck" framing was accidentally half-right for the wrong
  reason. The early Gorillaz test (2026-07-14) that needed ~120s to match
  likely worked *because* an uncapped fpcalc call reports the full file's
  metadata duration, which happened to be close to the true track length -
  not because 120s of audio content was inherently required. Longer
  clips were incidentally also sending better duration guesses.
- Requires no schema changes - the track durations this relies on were
  already being fetched and stored as part of ADR-002 layer 1. This fix
  was implementable with data the system already had; it just wasn't using
  it in the query itself, only for fuzzy-matching titles afterward.
- Meaningfully more AcoustID calls per identification attempt (one per
  candidate duration, up to ~12-14 for a typical album) instead of one.
  Acceptable for personal-use volume; pyacoustid already self-throttles
  `lookup()` calls.
- All three previously-failing albums (GNX, Grace, The Low End Theory) now
  match correctly through the real `identify_current_track()` pipeline
  using clips as short as 20 seconds, verified against the same digital
  files that exposed the bug.
- Still to validate: re-run this against real CD player line-in audio
  (not just clean digital rips) to confirm the fix holds outside the
  digital-file test case that found it.
